<exercises xml:id="ex-open-closed-interior" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Exercises related to open, closed sets, interior and closure of sets</title>
<p>
    We list a large number of small exercises for practice. Some of the exercises are taken from <sq>Elementary topology problem textbook</sq> by Viro et.al.
</p>
<exercisegroup>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            State whether the following statements are true or false. Justify your answers.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                Let <m>a,b\in\R</m>. The interval <m>(a,b)</m> is an open subset in <m>\R_\ell</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                One may approximate <m>a</m> from the right, for instance, by considering <m>a+1/n</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                Let <m>a,b\in\R</m>. The interval <m>[a,b)</m> is a closed subset of <m>\R</m> in the usual topology.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                If <m>[a,b)</m> were closed then, its complement <m>(-\infty,a)\cup[b,\infty)</m> will be open. However, any open interval of <m>\R</m> containing <m>b</m> will contain points other than <m>\R\setminus[a,b)</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                If the interval <m>[0,2]</m> is given the subspace topology from <m>\R</m> then, <m>[0,1)</m> is an open subset of <m>[0,2]</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                We have <m>[0,1)=(-1,1)\cap [0,2]</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                If <m>A</m> is a closed subset of <m>\R^2</m> then, <m>x+A=\{x+a:a\in A\}</m> is a closed subset of <m>\R^2</m> for every <m>x\in\R^2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Let <m>y\in\R^2</m> be a limit point of <m>x+A</m>. Thus, there exists a sequence <m>x+a_n\to y</m>, i.e., <m>a_n\to y-x</m>, i.e., <m>y-x</m> is a limit point of <m>A</m>. As <m>A</m> is closed, <m>y-x\in A</m>. Hence, there exists <m>a\in A</m> such that <m>a=y-x</m>, i.e., <m>y=x+a\in x+A</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>X=[0,1]\cup(2,3)</m> be given the subspace topology from <m>\R</m>. Consider the following subsets of <m>X</m>.
                <me>
                    Y=[0,1]\quad\text{and}\quad Z=(2,3)
                </me>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
        
        <choices>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Both <m>Y,Z</m> are closed but not open.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        The complement of <m>Y</m> (resp., <m>Z</m>) is open (resp., closed). Therefore, <m>Y</m> is closed (resp., open). Furthermore, as <m>Y=(-1,1.5)\cap X</m> (resp., <m>Z=[1.5,3.5]\cap X</m>), <m>Y</m> is open (resp., <m>Z</m> is closed.)
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Both <m>Y,Z</m> are open as well as closed.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        The complement of <m>Y</m> (resp., <m>Z</m>) is open (resp., closed). Therefore, <m>Y</m> is closed (resp., open). Furthermore, as <m>Y=(-1,1.5)\cap X</m> (resp., <m>Z=[1.5,3.5]\cap X</m>), <m>Y</m> is open (resp., <m>Z</m> is closed.)
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The <m>Y</m> is closed but not open, and <m>Z</m> is open but not closed.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        The complement of <m>Y</m> (resp., <m>Z</m>) is open (resp., closed). Therefore, <m>Y</m> is closed (resp., open). Furthermore, as <m>Y=(-1,1.5)\cap X</m> (resp., <m>Z=[1.5,3.5]\cap X</m>), <m>Y</m> is open (resp., <m>Z</m> is closed.)
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The <m>Z</m> is closed but not open, and <m>Y</m> is open but not closed.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        The complement of <m>Y</m> (resp., <m>Z</m>) is open (resp., closed). Therefore, <m>Y</m> is closed (resp., open). Furthermore, as <m>Y=(-1,1.5)\cap X</m> (resp., <m>Z=[1.5,3.5]\cap X</m>), <m>Y</m> is open (resp., <m>Z</m> is closed.)
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        
        </choices>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                Consider <m>X=\{(x,y)\in\R^2:x\geq 0\text{ and }y\geq 0\}</m> with the subspace topology induced from <m>\R^2</m>. Then, <m>Y=\{(x,y)\in X:0\leq x&lt;2\text{ and }0\leq y&lt;2\}</m> is not an open subset of <m>X</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>    
    <exercise>
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>
                    Let <m>(X,\tau)</m> be a nonempty topological space with the indiscrete topology, i.e., <m>\tau=\{\emptyset,X\}</m>. For <m>x\in X</m>, the closure of <m>\{x\}</m> is <m>X</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    If <m>X=\{x\}</m> then, we are done. So assume that there exists <m>y\in X</m> distinct from <m>x</m>. The only open set containing <m>y</m> is <m>X</m>, and thus, <m>y\in\overline{\{x\}}</m>.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                The interior of rational (resp., irrational) numbers is nonempty.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                One may use density of rational numbers to conclude that the interior of <m>\Q</m> is empty.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                If <m>A_n</m> are subsets of <m>\R</m> then, <me>
                    \left(\bigcap A_n\right)^o=\bigcap A_n^o.
                </me>
                 
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Consider sets <m>A_n=(-1/n,1+1/n)</m>. As <m>A_n</m> are open, <m>A_n^o=A_n</m> and hence, <m>\cap A_n^o=\cap A_n=[0,1]</m> which is a closed set. However, <m>\left(\cap A_n\right)^o=\left([0,1]\right)^o=(0,1)</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                The interior of <m>(0,1)</m> is empty and its closure is <m>\R</m> when <m>\R</m> is given the cofinite topology.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                The interior of a set <m>A</m> is the largest open set contained in <m>A</m>. In the cofinite topology, every nonempty open set has a finite complement. Therefore, if a nonempty set <m>U</m> is an interior of <m>(0,1)</m> then, <m>\R\setminus U</m> is a finite set. However, as <m>\R\setminus (0,1)\subseteq\R\setminus U</m>, and <m>\R\setminus (0,1)</m> is infinite, we must have <m>U=\emptyset</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Given any <m>x\in\R</m> and a nonempty open subset <m>U</m> containing <m>x</m>, <m>(0,1)\cap U\neq\emptyset</m> because <m>\R\setminus U</m> is a finite set. Therefore, <m>x\in\overline{(0,1)}</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
               There exists a set <m>A</m> such that <m>A,A^o,\overline{A},\overline{A^o}</m> are all pairwise distinct. 
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Consider <m>A=[0,1)\cup(1,2]\cup\left(\Q\cap [3,4]\right)\cup\{5\}</m> as a subset of <m>\R</m> with the usual topology. Here,
                <m>A^o=(0,1)\cup (1,2)</m>, <m>\overline{A}=[0,2]\cup[3,4]\cup\{5\}</m>, and <m>\overline{A^o}=[0,2]</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                Consider <m>X=[0,1]\cup\{2\}</m> as a metric space with the metric <m>d(x,y)=|x-y|</m>. Let <m>S_2(0)=\{x\in X:d(x,0)=2\}</m> (sphere in <m>X</m>) and let <m>B_2(0)=\{x\in X:d(x,0)&lt;2\}</m> (ball in <m>X</m>). Then <m>S_2(0)\cap \overline{B_2(0)}\neq\emptyset</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Here <m>B_2(0)=[0,1]</m> and <m>\overline{B_2(0)}=[0,1]</m>, while <m>S_2(0)=\{2\}</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                Let <m>X\subseteq\R</m> be given the subspace topology. If <m>X</m> is a discrete subspace then, <m>X</m> is countable.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                For any <m>x\in X</m> there exists an open interval <m>U_x</m> such that <m>U_x\cap X=\{x\}</m> as the induced topology on <m>X</m> is discrete. For <m>x\neq y</m> we can choose smaller intervals, say <m>x\in I_x</m> and <m>y\in I_y</m> such that <m>I_x\cap I_y=\emptyset</m>, <m>I_x\cap X=\{x\}</m>, and <m>I_y\cap X=\{y\}</m>. For each <m>x\in X</m> pick <m>q_x\in I_x\cap\Q</m>. Then, the map <m>X\to\Q</m> given by <m>x\mapsto q_x</m> is injective.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                Let <m>X</m> be a topological space. For any subset <m>A</m> of <m>X</m>, the boundary of <m>A</m> and the boundary of <m>\overline{A}</m> are the same.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Let <m>X</m> be the real numbers with cofinite topology. Suppose that <m>A=[0,1]</m>. For any <m>x\in\R</m> and an open subset <m>U_x</m> containing <m>x</m>. As <m>\R\setminus U_x</m> is finite, <m>U_x\cap [0,1]\neq\emptyset</m>. Hence, <m>\overline{A}=X</m>. Similar reasoning show that there are no nonempty open subsets in <m>A</m>, i.e., <m>A^o=\emptyset</m>. Therefore, the boundary of <m>A=\overline{A}\setminus A^o=X</m>, while the boundary of <m>\overline{A}</m> is <m>\emptyset</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                We remark that the above statement is <em>true</em> if <m>X</m> is assumed to be a Hausdorff space.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                Let <m>(X,d)</m> be a metric space. Suppose that <m>B_r(x), B_s(y)</m> be open balls of radii <m>r,s</m>, respectively. If <m>B_r(x)\subsetneq B_s(y)</m> then, <m>r&lt;2s</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Let <m>z\in B_s(y)\setminus B_r(x)</m>. Thus, <m>r\leq d(x,z)</m>. We thus have the following inequalities.
                <me>
                    r\leq d(x,z)\leq d(x,y)+d(y,z)&lt; 2s
                </me>
                
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>

</exercisegroup>
</exercises>